  "title": "Typical tactics and plans",
  "intro": "Five positions from the lines you have just learnt, each stopping on the move that matters. Play through the moves and find the last one.\n\n## What these are, and what they are not\n\nThree of the five end on a genuine tactic: a bishop that cannot be captured, a knight that defends a pawn by attacking it, and a pin that stays invisible until you move the piece holding it shut. The other two end on a decision, which is the more common kind of moment in a real game. Knowing that ...c4 is better than the queen trade will win you more games than any combination in this opening, and it is not a tactic.\n\n## The themes worth carrying away\n\nAn open file is a tactic waiting to happen. In the quiet lines Black recaptures on g6 with the h-pawn for ordinary structural reasons, and eight moves later that rook makes ...Bxh2+ unanswerable, because a king cannot capture a defended piece.\n\nDefend by attacking. In the Exchange Slav, ...Na5 guards b7 by attacking b7, so the queen that was going to take there would lose herself. The follow-up matters just as much: the check on a4 has to be blocked by the bishop, because only the bishop attacks the queen back.\n\nWatch the diagonal behind your own piece. In the 6.Ne5 line Black's knight on e5 is pinned against the queen on c7, and the pin is invisible until you move the knight and lose the queen.\n\nSpace before trades. Against the London, pushing the pawn to c4 keeps a queenside bind that a queen trade on b3 hands straight back.\n\nStrike the wedge. When White's pawn reaches e5 in the main line, ...f6 is the move that stops the position going quietly downhill.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nPlaying a sensible developing move in a position that has a forcing one in it. Trading queens because the trade was offered. Moving a piece that is holding a diagonal shut. And reaching a comfortable position and then having no plan, which is the most expensive mistake on this list.",
